<h2>Stage matters: the core of the decision</h2>
<p>NDPS jurisprudence contains strong accused-friendly rules — the bar on convicting upon confessions to officers chief among them. The temptation is to deploy those rules at every stage, including to kill a case before trial. The Supreme Court's answer in <em>Sadiq Pasha</em> is a stage-discipline rule: precedents generated at the conviction or bail-cancellation stage presuppose a record of evidence that a quashing court does not have. Where the chargesheet discloses a case to go to trial — a seizure of over 32 kilograms, two co-accused facing trial on the same transaction, and a named absconder — the High Court cannot weigh the eventual sufficiency of evidence in advance and discharge the function reserved to the trial court.</p>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Investigation / chargesheet.</strong> Questions of admissibility are noted, not decided; quashing lies only where no offence is disclosed at all.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Cognizance and charge.</strong> The accused may argue there is no material beyond an inadmissible confession — at cognizance or through a discharge application. This, the Court said, is where the respondent's contention belongs.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Trial.</strong> The bar on convicting upon Section 67 statements operates with full force; the prosecution must stand on legally admissible evidence.</div>
</div>

<h2>The absconder dimension</h2>
<p>Running through the judgment is a quieter point about conduct. The respondent never faced the process — he was an absconder when the chargesheet was filed, the case against him was split, and yet he obtained a quashing while his co-accused stood trial. The Supreme Court's directions reverse that asymmetry: appearance within two weeks, bail thereafter on the trial court's terms, and coercive steps on failure. Courts are consistently less receptive to pre-trial relief sought by a person who has evaded the court whose process he challenges; the path to every remedy in the criminal justice system runs through submission to it.</p>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>For the defence bar:</strong> the judgment does not shrink Section 528 BNSS (Section 482 CrPC) jurisdiction — it maps it. A quashing petition in an NDPS case must show that, taking the chargesheet at its highest, no triable case exists; an argument that the surviving evidence will prove too weak is a discharge argument, and dressing it as quashing now carries the risk of precisely this reversal, with the accused ordered to appear on a fixed clock.</p>
</div>

<h2>Reading it with the bail landscape</h2>
<p>There is a pragmatic consolation for accused persons in the Court's final directions: appearance brings a bail hearing, and the Court itself contemplated release on terms once the respondent submits. For an accused implicated mainly through a co-accused's statement, the realistic litigation sequence is appearance, bail, and a discharge application that puts the State to identifying material beyond the inadmissible confession. That sequence preserves every substantive protection NDPS law confers, while respecting the structure of the Sanhita — which is what the Supreme Court has insisted on here.</p>

<div class="check">
  <p>Key holdings at a glance: conviction-stage and bail-stage precedents do not control quashing petitions; confessions under Section 67 NDPS and disclosures under Section 53A cannot found a conviction, but their weakness is tested at trial or discharge, not by pre-trial quashing; and an absconding accused seeking relief must first submit to the court's process.</p>
</div>

